==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*ten-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|susteinen}}, {{m|enm|sustenen}}, from {{der|en|fro|sustenir}} ({{cog|fr|soutenir}}), from {{der|en|la|sustineō|sustineō, sustinēre|t=to uphold}}, from {{m|la|sub-|t=from below, up}} + {{m|la|teneō|t=hold|pos=verb}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/səˈsteɪn/|a=RP,GA,CA}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-sustain.ogg|a=California}}
* {{IPA|en|/səˈstæɪn/|a=AU,NZ}}
* {{hyph|en|sus|tain}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪn|s=2}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[maintain]], or keep in [[existence]].
#: {{ux|en|The professor had trouble '''sustaining''' students’ interest until the end of her lectures.}}
#: {{ux|en|The city came under '''sustained''' attack by enemy forces.}}
#: {{ux|en|Sam managed to '''sustain''' his erection for two straight hours.}}
#* {{RQ:Orwell Nineteen Eighty-Four|chapter=9|text=All the beliefs, habits, tastes, emotions, mental attitudes that characterize our time are really designed to '''sustain''' the mystique of the Party and prevent the true nature of present-day society from being perceived.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[provide]] for or [[nourish]].
#: {{syn|en|hold}}
#: {{ux|en|provisions to '''sustain''' an army}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Nehemiah|9|21|url=https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Nehemiah+9&version=KJV|text=Yea, forty years didst thou '''sustain''' them in the wilderness, so that they lacked nothing; their clothes waxed not old, and their feet swelled not.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[encourage]] or [[sanction]] (something). {{rfquote-sense|en}}
# {{senseid|en|experience or suffer}} {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[experience]] or [[suffer]] (''an [[injury]], etc.'').
#: {{cot|en|incur}}
#: {{ux|en|The building '''sustained''' major damage in the earthquake.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 8|III|2|text={{...}} if you omit<br>The offer of this time, I cannot promise<br>But that you shall '''sustain''' moe new disgraces,<br>With these you bear already.}}
#* {{RQ:Dryden Aeneis|7|line=592-593|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A65112.0001.001|text=Shall ''Turnus'' then such endless Toil '''sustain''',<br>In fighting Fields, and conquer Towns in vain:}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[confirm]], [[prove]], or [[corroborate]]; to [[uphold]].
#: {{ux|en|to '''sustain''' a charge, an accusation, or a proposition}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1876|author=w:Henry Martyn Robert|title={{w|Robert's Rules of Order|Pocket Manual of Rules of Order for Deliberative Assemblies}}|location=Chicago|publisher=Griggs|year_published=1885|section=Section 61 (e)|page=167|url=https://archive.org/details/pocketmanualrul00robegoog|text=After the vote is taken, the Chairman states that the decision of the Chair is '''sustained''', or reversed, as the case may be.}}
# {{lb|en|law|of a judge}} To allow, accept, or admit (e.g. an objection or motion) as valid.
#: {{ant|en|overrule}}
# To keep from falling; to [[bear]]; to [[uphold]]; to [[support]].
#: {{ux|en|A foundation '''sustains''' the superstructure; an animal '''sustains''' a load; a rope '''sustains''' a weight.}}
# To [[aid]], [[comfort]], or [[relieve]]; to [[vindicate]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare King Lear|III|3|text=When I desir’d their leave that I might pity him, they took from me the use of mine own house, charg’d me on pain of perpetual displeasure neither to speak of him, entreat for him, nor any way '''sustain''' him.}}
#* {{RQ:Dryden Aeneis|book=6|line=1122-1123|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A65112.0001.001|text=His Sons, who seek the Tyrant to '''sustain''',<br>And long for Arbitrary Lords again,}}
# {{lb|en|music|of a drum or its sound}} To be a long-lasting sound.
#: {{ux|en|I like how long that drum '''sustains'''.}}

